from helpers import *

def part1(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p1"><small>Part 1</small>The Essential Tool Kit</h1>']
    h.append('<p>When factories and stores stop, the tools you already own — and the ones you can collect in the first months — are what let you build shelters, fences and water systems, grow food, and repair everything else. Hand tools last for generations if they are good steel and kept sharp, dry and properly handled. Power tools are a bonus while fuel or batteries last; plan as if every job will eventually be done by hand.</p>')
    h.append('<div class="kpi"><div><b>Axe, saw, file</b>the three tools that do the most work per pound</div><div><b>100+ years</b>life of a good forged axe head with care</div><div><b>~2–5 handles</b>a working axe may wear out in a lifetime</div><div><b>1 file</b>sharpens dozens of tools — stockpile many</div></div>')
    h.append('<h2 id="kit">1.1 What to stockpile or salvage first</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Prioritize tools that are <b>hard to make</b> (files, saws, drill bits, hardened steel), <b>wear out</b> (files, sandpaper, whetstones, saw blades) or are <b>needed for food and water</b> (shovels, hoes, axes). Tools that are easy to make in a forge (hooks, hinges, nails, simple tongs) are a lower priority than the hardened tools that make the forge work possible.</p>')
    h.append(table(["Priority", "Tool", "Why it matters / what to look for", "Supports volume"], [
        ["<b>1</b>", "Axes: felling axe (3–4 lb), splitting maul, hatchet", "Firewood, clearing, building, hewing. Look for forged heads with no cracks around the eye. Hickory or ash handles.", "Electricity (fuel), House building"],
        ["<b>1</b>", "Files: mill bastard 8–10 in, axe file, slim taper (saw) files, rasps", "Files sharpen everything and cannot be made without a hardening shop. Buy and salvage MANY; keep dry in paper.", "All"],
        ["<b>1</b>", "Saws: bow saw + spare blades, crosscut handsaw, rip saw, two-person crosscut, hacksaw + many blades", "Cutting firewood, lumber, poles. Saw blades and hacksaw blades are hard to make — stockpile.", "House building"],
        ["<b>1</b>", "Shovels and spades (round point, square, trenching), mattock/pick, digging bar", "Wells, latrines, gardens, footings, ditches. Forged one-piece heads last longest.", "Water, Food, Concrete"],
        ["<b>2</b>", "Hammers: 16–20 oz claw, 2–3 lb cross-peen, sledge (8–12 lb), mallet", "Building, forging, splitting with wedges. A 2½ lb cross-peen is the starting forging hammer.", "House building, Blacksmithing"],
        ["<b>2</b>", "Hand drills: brace + auger bits, egg-beater drill + twist bits, breast drill", "Drilling without electricity. Auger bits for pegged joinery; twist bits for metal.", "House building"],
        ["<b>2</b>", "Wrenches and pliers: combination set, adjustable, pipe wrenches, vise-grips, screwdrivers", "Repairing pumps, vehicles, windmills, plumbing.", "Water, Electricity"],
        ["<b>2</b>", "Garden tools: hoes, rakes, scythe + snath, sickle, pitchfork, broadfork, pruning saw", "Food production at scale without fuel.", "Food"],
        ["<b>3</b>", "Woodworking: chisels (¼–1½ in), drawknife, spokeshave, froe, block plane, adze, levels, squares", "Joinery, handles, shingles, furniture.", "House building"],
        ["<b>3</b>", "Metalworking: bench vise (or post vise), cold chisels, punches, tap & die set, tin snips, rivets", "Repairs and making parts.", "All"],
        ["<b>3</b>", "Sharpening: combination whetstones (coarse/fine), diamond plate, axe puck, honing oil", "Keeps everything else working.", "All"]]))
    h.append('<h2 id="salvage">1.2 Where to salvage tools and steel</h2>')
    h.append('<p>In a long-term grid-down situation, many tools sit unused in abandoned or damaged buildings. Always respect ownership and local agreements — trade, ask, or work with community leaders; scavenging from the living is theft and gets people hurt. Places that typically hold the most useful material:</p>')
    h.append(table(["Source", "Look for"], [
        ["Hardware stores, farm-supply and lumber yards", "Files, saw blades, hacksaw blades, drill bits, handles, wedges, rivets, wire, bar stock, nails, bolts, sandpaper, whetstones, linseed oil"],
        ["Farms and ranches", "Anvils, post vises, forges (many old farms had a shop), hand tools, leaf springs, disc harrow blades, plow points, tines, chain, scythes, fencing tools"],
        ["Home garages and basements", "Hand-tool sets, socket sets, clamps, levels, bench vises, battery chargers, small welders, grinders, files"],
        ["Auto shops, scrapyards, vehicle lots", "Leaf and coil springs, axles, brake drums (forge bodies), torsion bars, jacks, compressors, welding gear, wire rope"],
        ["Railroads (abandoned lines)", "Rail (anvils), tie plates, spikes, track bolts, joint bars"],
        ["Construction sites", "Rebar, wheelbarrows, picks, digging bars, shovels, ladders, scaffolding clamps, generators"],
        ["Schools, colleges, fairgrounds", "Shop classes: anvils, vises, forges, drill presses, hand tools; agricultural programs"],
        ["Historic sites, museums, antique stores", "Hand tools that were made to be used without power — braces, planes, froes, drawknives, crosscut saws, blacksmith tools"]]))
    h.append(caution("Quality check before you carry it home", "Reject axe and hammer heads with cracks at the eye or chipped, mushroomed faces (they can shed fragments). Ring-test anvils: tap with a hammer; a good steel face rings and the hammer bounces strongly. Check files by dragging across glass or a hardened bolt: a good file bites. Rust is usually fine — pitting deep enough to weaken the edge is not."))
    h.append(check("Grab-first checklist (one person, one vehicle load)", [
        "2 felling axes, 1 maul, 1 hatchet, 4 spare handles + wedges", "12+ mill bastard files, 6 slim taper saw files, 2 rasps, file card (brush)",
        "Bow saw + 10 blades; crosscut and rip handsaws; hacksaw + 50 blades", "3 shovels, 1 spade, 1 mattock, 1 digging bar, 2 hoes, 1 rake",
        "Claw hammer, 2½ lb cross-peen, 10 lb sledge, 2 splitting wedges", "Brace + auger bit set; hand drill + twist bits (1/16–1/2 in)",
        "Combination wrench set, 2 adjustable, 2 pipe wrenches, vise-grips, screwdrivers, pliers", "Chisel set, drawknife, froe, block plane, carpenter’s square, level, chalk line",
        "Whetstones (coarse/fine), diamond plate, axe puck, oil", "Bench or post vise; anvil or heavy steel block; any tongs and hammers from a farm shop",
        "Boiled linseed oil, paste wax, gun oil or lanolin; sandpaper and steel wool", "Safety glasses, leather gloves, hearing protection, first-aid kit with burn dressings"]))
    return "\n".join(h)

def part2(D):
    h = ['<h1 class="part" id="p2"><small>Part 2</small>Tool Care: Sharpening, Rust, Handles &amp; Saws</h1>']
    h.append('<p>A dull tool is slow and dangerous: it glances off wood, takes more force, and tires you out. Keeping tools sharp, rust-free and tightly handled is a daily skill in a grid-down world. The Forest Service ax and saw manuals in the sources section are the best free references on this subject — print them.</p>')
    h.append('<h2 id="sharpen">2.1 Sharpening</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Files</b> remove metal fast and are used on softer tools: axes, mauls, shovels, hoes, machetes, mower blades, saw teeth. A file cuts only on the <b>push stroke</b> — lift it on the return or you dull it. Clean clogged teeth with a file card. Push the file <b>into</b> the edge (toward the cutting edge) with the tool clamped, and wear gloves. <b>Whetstones</b> (natural or man-made, oil or water) finish what the file starts and sharpen hardened tools a file cannot cut — knives, chisels, plane irons. Work coarse → fine: coarse (~200–400 grit) for repair, medium (~800–1000) for sharpening, fine (3000+) for polishing. Natural stones: Arkansas novaculite (Ozarks/Ouachitas) is one of the best in the world; fine sandstone, slate and river stones also work.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.sharpening_angles(), "<b>Figure 1. Typical edge angles.</b> “Per side” is the angle each bevel makes with the centerline; “included” is the total angle of the edge."))
    h.append(table(["Tool", "Sharpen with", "Edge / method", "Notes"], [
        ["Felling axe", "File, then puck or stone", "Convex “appleseed” bevel, 2–3 in back from edge", "File toward the edge from each side equally; keep the original curve of the bit"],
        ["Splitting maul, wedges", "File or grinder", "Blunt (≈ 60°+)", "Should not be razor-sharp — it splits, not cuts. Grind off mushroomed polls"],
        ["Hatchet, machete, brush hook", "File, then stone", "20–30° per side", "Steeper for brush and bone"],
        ["Knives", "Stones, ceramic rod, leather strop", "15–25° per side", "Raise a burr along the whole edge, then remove it with alternating light strokes"],
        ["Chisels, plane irons", "Stones (flat!)", "25° bevel + 30° micro-bevel", "Back must be flat and polished; flatten stones on sandpaper on glass or concrete"],
        ["Shovels, hoes, spades", "File (mill bastard)", "30–45°, one bevel on inner/upper face", "A sharp shovel cuts roots and sod; touch up every few hours of use"],
        ["Scythe", "Peening jig/anvil + whetstone", "Edge is thinned by hammer (peened), then honed", "Hone every 10–15 min of mowing with a wet stone; peen a few times a season"],
        ["Drill bits (twist)", "Fine grinding wheel, stone", "118° point, 12–15° lip clearance", "Both lips equal length or the hole runs oversize"],
        ["Cold chisels, punches", "File or grinder", "60–70° included", "Grind off any mushrooming on the struck end"]]))
    h.append(caution("Do not overheat hardened tools on a grinder", "If the edge turns straw, blue or purple while grinding, you have drawn the temper and softened it — the edge will not hold. Grind with light pressure and cool constantly in water; better yet, file and stone by hand. A pedal-powered or hand-cranked grindstone (sandstone wheel running in water) is the traditional way."))
    h.append('<h2 id="rust">2.2 Rust prevention and cleaning</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Rust is the main enemy of stored tools, especially in humid regions. Extension services give the same basic routine: clean off soil and sap after each use, dry, oil, and store dry and off the floor.</p>')
    h.append('<ul><li><b>Daily:</b> knock off soil, wipe dry, wipe metal with an oily rag (keep one in a can by the door). A bucket of coarse sand moistened with vegetable oil or clean motor oil (not used oil, which is toxic) lets you plunge shovels and hoes in to clean and oil them in one move.</li><li><b>Remove rust:</b> wire brush, steel wool, sandpaper, or a block of pumice; for heavy rust soak in vinegar 12–24 hours, scrub, neutralize in baking-soda water, dry and oil immediately.</li><li><b>Protective coatings:</b> light machine oil, gun oil, lanolin, paste wax, beeswax/tallow rubbed on warm metal, or boiled linseed oil (dries to a thin film). For long storage, coat in grease or wax and wrap in oiled paper.</li><li><b>Storage:</b> hang tools on a wall away from concrete floors (which wick moisture); keep files separated so their teeth do not touch; store saws with a slotted wood guard on the teeth.</li></ul>')
    h.append(danger("Linseed-oil rags can catch fire by themselves", "Rags soaked in boiled or raw linseed oil heat up as they dry and can ignite spontaneously. Spread them flat outdoors to dry, or put them in a metal can full of water with a lid."))
    h.append('<h2 id="handles">2.3 Handles and rehafting</h2>')
    h.append('<p><b>Best handle woods</b> are tough, shock-resistant hardwoods: <b>hickory</b> (the North American standard), <b>white ash</b>, hop-hornbeam (ironwood), black locust, white oak, sugar maple, persimmon and Osage orange. Choose straight-grained split wood (not sawn across the grain) from the trunk, free of knots. Split a log into quarters with wedges, rough-shape with a hatchet, and let the blank dry slowly for months (indoors, out of the sun; seal the ends with wax, paint or glue) before final shaping. A handle shaved green will shrink and loosen in the head.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.axe_rehafting(), "<b>Figure 2. Rehafting an axe.</b> The eye of a North American axe is tapered: narrow at the bottom, wider at the top. The wedge spreads the handle into that flare so the head cannot fly off."))
    h.append('<ol><li><b>Remove the old handle:</b> saw it off just below the head, drill several holes into the wood left in the eye, then drive the plug <b>out through the top</b> (the wide end) with a drift or a large bolt. Never burn out an old handle — heat can draw the temper of the head.</li><li><b>Fit:</b> shave the top of the new handle with a rasp, spokeshave or knife until the head slides on about two-thirds of the way. Smoke the eye with a candle or rub with pencil graphite; tap on, remove, and shave only the shiny/marked high spots. Repeat until the head seats with the handle standing ¼–½ in (6–12 mm) above the top of the eye.</li><li><b>Saw the kerf</b> (slot) for the wedge if the handle lacks one: down the long axis of the eye, two-thirds to three-quarters of the eye depth.</li><li><b>Seat the head:</b> hold the handle vertical, head down but not touching anything, and strike the end of the handle (the knob) with a mallet. The head’s inertia drives it onto the handle.</li><li><b>Wedge:</b> make a dry hardwood wedge as long as the eye and slightly thicker than the kerf. Glue optional. Drive it until it stops moving, then saw off the excess ⅛ in (3 mm) above the head. A steel wedge driven across it at 45° adds grip.</li><li><b>Finish:</b> sand, then soak the wood with boiled linseed oil (several coats over days). Do not varnish or paint the grip — it causes blisters and hides cracks. Soaking a loose head in water only works briefly and crushes wood fibers; re-wedge or rehang instead.</li></ol>')
    h.append(tip("Other handles", "Hammer and hatchet handles fit the same way. Shovel and hoe handles fit into a socket: taper the end to match, drive in, and fix with a single screw or rivet. Files must have a handle — a bare tang can stab into the palm. Any short piece of drilled hardwood or a corncob works."))
    h.append('<h2 id="saws">2.4 Saw setting and filing</h2>')
    h.append('<p>Handsaws need two things to cut well: sharp teeth and enough <b>set</b> (teeth bent alternately left and right) so the kerf is wider than the blade and the saw does not bind. Rip saws cut with the grain using chisel-shaped teeth; crosscut saws cut across the grain using knife-pointed teeth. Clamp the saw in a saw vise (two boards in a bench vise work) with the teeth just above the jaws.</p>')
    h.append(fig(D.saw_teeth(), "<b>Figure 3. Rip and crosscut teeth and set.</b> Use a slim triangular taper file sized to the teeth per inch (TPI)."))
    h.append(table(["Saw", "Teeth", "Filing", "Set"], [
        ["Rip handsaw", "4½–7 TPI", "File straight across (90° to blade), 0–5° rake", "Moderate; less for dry hardwood"],
        ["Crosscut handsaw", "7–12 TPI", "Rake ≈ 15°, fleam 20–25°; file every other tooth from one side, then turn the saw around", "Moderate; more for green softwood"],
        ["Bow saw / buck saw", "Peg or raker teeth", "Usually replaceable blades — stockpile them; raker teeth can be filed like a crosscut saw", "—"],
        ["Two-person crosscut", "Cutter teeth + rakers", "File cutters to a sharp point; joint, then file rakers 0.010–0.030 in (0.25–0.75 mm) below cutters (lower for soft wood)", "Small: start about 0.010 in (0.25 mm) and increase only if the saw binds"],
        ["Hacksaw", "18–32 TPI", "Not filed — replace blades (stockpile hundreds)", "Built in"]]))
    h.append(tip("Signs your saw needs work", "Saw drifts to one side → uneven set (stone the heavy side lightly). Saw binds in the cut → not enough set. Cut is slow but straight → dull teeth. Teeth uneven height → joint first. File every tooth with the same number of strokes and the same pressure."))
    return "\n".join(h)
